Chapter: CharAtPastEnd
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 An index AT the length must trap, not just one beyond it. There is no byte
 at index length; a zero-length substring at that position is legal and a
 character read is not, and that one-off is the easiest thing to get wrong.

 Before the guard this read into the next allocation: with a neighbour
 filled with `Z`, char-code-at at index 20 on a five-byte text answered 64.

 This is the second of the two char-index guards; the first is a negative
 index, in codex/test/char-at-negative.codex.

 `.fatal`: dies on UD2. The battery-visible half is
 codex/test/ops/char-at-bounds.codex.

Section: Entry

  s : Text = "abcde"

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("got=" & show (char-code-at s (text-length s)))
  end

